INT. MADAME ROUSSEL'S SALON - PARIS - EVENING (1791)

Candles in silver sconces. Gilt chairs. A harpsichord with its lid closed. Six guests sit around a tea table, porcelain cups trembling on saucers, as the window shows a red dusk beyond the curtains.

MADAME ROUSSEL, 50s, presides in powdered hair. Her husband, MONSIEUR ROUSSEL, 60s, studies the sugar tongs. Their niece, LOUISE, 18, watches the window. A young poet, ANTOINE, 20s, fiddles with a cravat pin. A priest, ABBÉ FOURNIER, 60s, stirs his tea long past need. An officer, CAPTAIN DUVALIER, 30s, sits stiffly, hand on his sword.

MADAME ROUSSEL
More sugar, Abbé?

ABBÉ FOURNIER
No, Madame, I—

LOUISE
Did you hear that?

ANTOINE
It's only the carriage.

MONSIEUR ROUSSEL
At this hour there are no carriages.

CAPTAIN DUVALIER
(rising)
I will see.

MADAME ROUSSEL
Sit down, Captain.

ABBÉ FOURNIER
Child, it is nothing.

LOUISE
It is a drum.

Silence. The tea steams. Far below, a drumbeat rolls through the street. Then, much closer, a fist on the front door. Madame Roussel pours another cup with perfect, trembling grace.
